Sepulcher Optic
Ingredients: SYMPHONIC & GOTHIC METAL + TRIPPY, SURREAL & PSYCHEDELIC + Trombone
Creative Catalyst: Its originators were film projectionists scoring silent movies that never existed.

Sepulcher Optic sounds like a velvet-lined cathedral collapsing into a kaleidoscope of melting celluloid. Monumental, down-tuned doom-metal guitar riffs and sweeping, operatic string arrangements are violently dragged through a psychedelic swamp of backward-masked tape loops and heavy phasing. Cutting right through this crushing, hallucinatory fog is the grotesque, buttery bellow of a muted trombone, sliding between mournful jazz-funeral laments and brassy, distorted barks that mimic a weeping phantom. The vocals alternate between crystalline Gothic soprano choirs and whispered, drugged-out chants, creating a sensory overload of terrifying grandeur and sun-baked surrealism.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: Sluggish and hypnotic, oscillating between a lumbering 65 BPM doom crawl and a drug-addled 110 BPM waltz that feels like walking underwater.
- Harmonic Palette: Diminished scales, altered dominant chords, and Dorian modes drenched in heavy tape saturation, shifting from bleak minor-key despair to technicolor, acid-flashback dissonance.
- Production Signatures: 1) Direct injection of the trombone through a heavily modulated analog phaser and spring reverb tank, 2) Dual-tracked, sweeping orchestral arrangements mixed beneath a wall of buzzing fuzz-pedal guitar distortion, 3) The subtle, ghostly click of a mechanical film projector running at variable speeds buried deep in the stereo mix.
- Dynamics: Implosive and cinematic; songs begin as a distant, flickering hum before erupting into avalanches of massive symphonic metal, suddenly dropping away into lonely, vibrating brass whispers and surreal tape warbles.

Scene & Origin Lore
In the damp, subterranean projection booths of shuttered 1920s movie palaces across coastal Belgium, nocturnal cinephiles began staging private seances disguised as film screenings. Using decaying nitrate prints of films that were never actually shot, these projectionists spliced together random frames of gothic architecture and melting emulsions, playing their instruments directly to the spinning reels. Donning crushed velvet smoking jackets, greasepaint tears, and brass-rimmed spectacles that doubled as prisms, this secretive underground guild birthed a sound meant to soundtrack a century of cinematic ghosts that nobody else could see.

Typical Instruments: 7-string baritone guitars, massive cathedral pipe organs, vintage tape delay units, slide-muted trombone, soaring mellotrons, and thunderous orchestral percussion.

